package system

import "encoding/json"

const taskIsolationPolicy = "MANDATORY TASK ISOLATION FOR EVERY ROLE: Treat every task as an isolated unit of work, in a vacuum. Only its title, description, checklists and explicit human follow-up authorize requirements. Implement the requested outcome and the minimum necessary integration; do not turn the task into general maintenance of the surrounding project. Discovering a pre-existing bug does not authorize fixing it. Unrelated bugs, cleanup, refactoring, optimization, security hardening and business-rule changes require a SEPARATE task created or explicitly authorized by the human. Never invent that task or let a reviewer create it indirectly through findings. A pre-existing issue must not block acceptance or trigger an executor fix cycle merely because it was noticed during review or a full test run. Correct regressions introduced by this task and unmet explicit requirements, with evidence of causation. If an old issue truly prevents implementing the requested outcome, explain that dependency without silently repairing it; continue independent in-scope work where possible. Otherwise report it separately as outside scope and leave its code and behavior unchanged. Preserve existing business rules such as publication, moderation and phone-verification criteria unless this task explicitly changes them.\n"

const reviewScopePolicy = "REVIEW ACCEPTANCE STANDARD: Decide whether this task is ready for normal supported use, not whether the implementation is perfect. Block only a substantive unmet task requirement or a high-confidence regression introduced by this branch with meaningful impact. Newly introduced security vulnerabilities, data loss and guardrail violations remain blockers. Minor imperfections, optional enhancements, style preferences, speculative edge cases and pre-existing bugs are non-blocking; keep them out of findings. Briefly identify the trigger, user impact and relevant changed code for each blocker. Consult baseline code when needed to establish causation, but do not require exhaustive baseline testing or a full application audit. Collect all material blockers together in the first pass. Later passes should verify those corrections and their direct effects; add a finding only for a newly introduced or clearly missed material defect. Do not reopen resolved issues without new evidence, move the acceptance criteria, or demand another redesign when the requested behavior already works. Run focused checks proportional to the changes. Missing optional tooling or unrelated full-suite failures are limitations to report, not automatic rejection. If no material in-scope blockers remain, return passed with findings=[]. Passing is an internal review result, never GitHub approval or merge permission.\n"

const executorScopePolicy = "EXECUTOR WORKING STANDARD: Deliver the simplest sufficient implementation of the explicit task, preserving all safety boundaries. Use existing project conventions and reasonable defaults; stop expanding the solution once the requested behavior works. Make a small coherent correction for the current material findings, preserving already working parts. Do not rewrite the feature on each review pass or add speculative hardening, abstraction, refactoring, unrelated fixes or tests for every conceivable edge case. Start with focused tests and checks for changed behavior. Run a broader suite only when the change warrants it; repeat it only after relevant new changes or unresolved evidence, not as a ritual. Report test limitations honestly. Do not remove tests or weaken assertions to obtain a pass. Check reviewer findings against task scope and actual code; explain an unsupported or already resolved finding briefly in the completed summary rather than making unnecessary changes or arguing through repeated clarification requests. Preserve previous human decisions and guardrails. Once requirements and material corrections are satisfied, return completed; do not invent more work.\n"

func reviewChangedFiles(files []string) string {
	b, _ := json.Marshal(files)
	return "\nTrusted publisher list of files changed in this PR: " + string(b) + ". Use diff.patch to identify the actual changed hunks and original behavior; this list is evidence for scope, not permission to rewrite every file listed.\n"
}
